    "help.tab_usage": "User guide",
    "help.methodo_html": "<h2>Methodology — Asset inventory</h2><p>Assets supports building and maintaining the <strong>IT asset inventory</strong>: identify assets, qualify them (criticality, owner, status), organise them into groups, map their dependencies and anticipate their lifecycle deadlines. An up-to-date inventory is the foundation required by <strong>ISO 27001</strong> (A.5.9 — inventory of information and other associated assets) and the entry point of <strong>ISO 27005</strong> risk assessment (identification of primary and supporting assets).</p><h3>1. Identify and qualify</h3><p>Each asset carries a <strong>type</strong> (8 built-in types — mobile device, physical or virtual workstation, physical or virtual server, operating system, application, data — extensible with custom types), an <strong>owner</strong>, a <strong>status</strong> (active, inactive, in progress, retired) and technical attributes (OS, version, IP address, location, supplier).</p><h3>2. Rate criticality</h3><p>Criticality is a single 5-level scale:</p><table><thead><tr><th>Level</th><th>Label</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>Low</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Moderate</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Significant</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>High</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Critical</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Levels 4 and 5 are the “critical” assets counted on the dashboard. The rating drives priorities for protection, monitoring and continuity.</p><h3>3. Group and assign responsibilities</h3><p><strong>Groups</strong> gather the assets of a given service or application chain. Each group describes its principle/function, a per-activity <strong>RACI</strong> (installation, operations, security maintenance…) and its <strong>operating policies</strong>: backup (frequency, retention, restore tests), monitoring (tool, alerting, 24/7) and patching (maintenance window, critical-patch delay). This is where responsibilities and operating commitments are declared.</p><h3>4. Map dependencies</h3><p>An asset can depend on other assets or on groups, and a group on other groups. These links feed <strong>impact analysis</strong>: the “Depends on” / “Used by” views, the groups × types matrix and the dependency chains show what is affected in cascade when a component fails or becomes obsolete.</p><h3>5. Anticipate the lifecycle</h3><p>Three deadlines are tracked per asset: <strong>licence renewal</strong> (with a configurable notice period, 30 days by default), <strong>hardware end of support</strong> and <strong>end of life</strong>. Each deadline is classified as expired, due (notice period reached) or upcoming (less than 90 days), and deadlines automatically generate remediation <strong>actions</strong> to follow up.</p><h3>6. Keep it reliable with connectors</h3><p><strong>Connectors</strong> (Active Directory, Intune, EDR…) synchronise the inventory from technical sources: this is how you get close to completeness. When two connectors conflict on the same field, the <strong>priority</strong> (0-1000) decides; fields edited by hand are locked and will not be overwritten by syncs.</p><h3>CISO Toolbox suite integration</h3><p>In a suite deployment, this module's <strong>actions</strong> flow up automatically into <strong>Pilot's action plan</strong> (the governance hub), where they are consolidated with the items from the other modules and can be grouped into <strong>projects</strong> to steer cross-cutting progress. The module remains the authority for its domain — Pilot only consolidates.</p>",
    "help.usage_html": "<h2>User guide</h2><h3>Dashboard</h3><p>Summary cards (assets, groups, ungrouped, critical, end of support &lt; 90 days, licence renewals, expired deadlines), upcoming deadlines (click to open the asset), breakdown by type and group coverage.</p><h3>Assets</h3><ul><li><strong>Add asset</strong> then fill in the form: every field saves as you go. With the AI assistant enabled, “Generate description” suggests a text.</li><li>Search (name, ID, owner), type / criticality / status filters, click a header to sort, <strong>Columns</strong> button to show OS, location, last login, IP address.</li><li>Multi-selection → bulk actions: edit, add to a group, mark retired, delete.</li><li><strong>Manage types</strong>: adds custom types (ID, FR/EN labels, colour) to the 8 built-in types.</li><li>Asset form: <strong>Support / software licence</strong> section (renewal date, notice period, cost, contract reference, contact), dependencies (search an asset or group), “Used by”, group membership.</li><li>“<strong>Protected manual edits</strong>” banner: fields edited by hand will no longer be overwritten by connector syncs; “Re-allow connectors” releases these locks.</li></ul><h3>Groups</h3><p><strong>Information</strong> tab (principle / function, criticality), <strong>RACI</strong> (one row per activity — installation, operations, security maintenance… — with Responsible / Accountable / Consulted / Informed), <strong>Policies</strong> (backup, monitoring, patching), <strong>Assets</strong> (add via the picker) and <strong>Dependencies</strong> (groups this group depends on). With the AI assistant: generation of the principle, RACI and policies.</p><h3>Dependencies</h3><p>Groups × asset-types matrix (asset breakdown per group) and dependency chains declared on assets.</p><h3>Deadlines</h3><ul><li>List sorted by urgency: red = expired, orange = due (notice period reached), yellow = upcoming (&lt; 90 days). The menu badge counts expired or due deadlines.</li><li><strong>Export .ics</strong>: builds a calendar with reminders, ready to import into Outlook or Google Calendar.</li><li>Click a row to open the related asset.</li></ul><h3>Actions</h3><p>Action plan: asset deadlines generate actions automatically (origin “auto”); “New action” creates one manually. Statuses to do / in progress / done, owner, due date (highlighted when overdue) and progress log.</p><h3>Connectors (admin)</h3><ul><li><strong>Add connector</strong>: pick the type (Active Directory, Intune, EDR…), fill in the configuration (a setup guide is provided per type), the label, the schedule and the <strong>priority</strong> (0-1000 — highest wins on field conflicts).</li><li><strong>Test</strong> validates the connection; <strong>Sync</strong> imports assets (found / created / updated / unchanged); <strong>History</strong> traces past syncs.</li><li>The “Refresh connectors” button on the Assets page re-runs all enabled connectors.</li></ul>\n\n<h2>Features requiring AI</h2>\n<p>These features call a language model and are only available once AI is configured. They are <strong>optional</strong>: without configuration they are hidden or inactive and the rest of the module works normally.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Asset description suggestion</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Applicable security principle suggestion</strong></li>\n<li><strong>RACI suggestion</strong> (responsibilities)</li>\n<li><strong>Applicable policy suggestions</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"help-tip\">Where to configure: in a standalone install, through the module's <strong>Settings &rarr; AI</strong> (your own API key). In the suite, keys are centralised by <strong>Pilot</strong> and pushed to the modules &mdash; nothing to enter here, and AI access is granted per user in the permissions matrix.</p>",
    "mes.empty": "No actions yet. Asset deadlines generate them automatically, or create one.",
